  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 222 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge that has been in place the past few days is \nlikely to stay nearly stationary over the next week. Throughout the \nweek, Guidance is showing there a few shortwave troughs that will \nmove through the region. \n\nMonday looks to be the hottest day of the week with highs reaching \nthe low 100s. The highest temperatures that could be seen are 105-\n107 degrees. These temperatures look to remain east of KS Hwy 27. \nDue to these high temperatures a Heat Advisory is in place for all \nof the Kansas and Nebraska Counties at this time. Winds look to \nmainly remain from the north-northeast with gusts up to 20-25 kts. \n\nThere are some precipation chances on Monday afternoon/evening. \nLooking at PoPs there is a 30-45% for the majority of the County \nWarning Area (CWA). Looking at Guidance, there does seem to be a \nshortwave and a boundary that will move in from the north and \ncontinue south. However some disagreement on the boundary itself.\nThe ECMWF and Canadian both have the boundary, but the GFS does not. \nAs for Precipitation there is agreement with the NBM and LREF that \nthere is a 20-30% chance of exceeding 0.10\" of precipation, for Yuma \nCounty in Colorado. Looking at ingredients needed for severe storms \nthere are some indications there could be storms but on the marginal \nside.\n\n\nMoving to the rest of the week, starting Tuesday temperatures begin \nto \"cool\" down to the high 90s. Precipitation chances return again \nin the afternoon and evening with PoP's reaching up to 60% in Yuma \nCounty, in Colorado. For the majority of the rest of the CWA there \nis values in the 20-40% range. For the extended part of the week, \nthe highs still remain in the 90s, with the exception of Thursday \nhighs cool down to the high 80s and low 90s. PoPs each day hangout \naround 20-40% for the majority of the CWA. One feature to keep an \neye on is on Thursday there looks to be a shortwave with an \nassociated surface low that could bring more weather chances. \nHowever, given this is almost a week out a lot can change.",
